{
  "slug": "brouwer-fixed-point-oq-01-oq-03-oq-02",
  "title": "Formalize the degree-theoretic proof in BorsukUlam",
  "phase": "OBSERVE",
  "status": "available",
  "tier": "B",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\text{No continuous odd map } g : S^n \\to S^{n-1} \\text{ exists } (n \\ge 1); \\text{ equivalently, } \\nexists\\, g : S^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n \\text{ continuous with } g(-x) = -g(x) \\text{ and } g(x) \\neq 0 \\text{ for all } x \\in S^n.",
    "plain": "The n-dimensional Borsuk-Ulam theorem is proved in the gallery, but its topological heart is currently assumed as the axiom no_continuous_odd_nonzero_on_sphere: that no continuous odd map from the n-sphere to the (n-1)-sphere exists. This problem asks for a machine-checked proof of that single obstruction in Lean 4 / Mathlib so the axiom can be replaced by a theorem and the parent entry upgraded toward verified status.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The odd-map obstruction is exactly where Borsuk-Ulam stops being elementary: n=1 needs only the IVT, but n>=2 requires that odd self-maps of the sphere have odd degree and are never null-homotopic.",
      "Removing this axiom would turn Borsuk-Ulam, No-Retraction, and the equivalence chain to the Brouwer Fixed Point theorem from conditionally proved into fully machine-checked results.",
      "A formal proof would establish reusable Mathlib-level infrastructure (odd maps, degree parity, or a Z/2 index) for future algebraic-topology formalizations."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [],
    "open": [
      "The axiom no_continuous_odd_nonzero_on_sphere in Proofs/BorsukUlam.lean is unproved for n >= 2.",
      "Mathlib does not currently expose a ready-made 'odd maps have odd degree' or 'no odd map S^n -> S^{n-1}' lemma in directly usable form."
    ],
    "goal": "Prove no_continuous_odd_nonzero_on_sphere (equivalently, no continuous odd map S^n -> S^{n-1}) in Lean 4 with Mathlib for all n >= 1, and replace the axiom declaration in Proofs/BorsukUlam.lean with a theorem; the parent's derived corollaries already follow from it."
  },
